---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';

const examples = [
  ['Ще не вмерла України ні слава, ні воля', 'Šče ne vmerła Ukraïny ni słava, ni vola'],
  ['Щастя не купиш за гроші', 'Ščasťa ne kupyš za hroši'],
  ['Чернівці, Житомир, Львів', 'Černivci, Žytomyr, Lviv'],
];
---

<Base lang="en" title="Ukrainian in the Latin alphabet"
      description="Evropéïća — a Latin orthography for Ukrainian, designed as a second script alongside Cyrillic.">
  <h1>Ukrainian in the Latin alphabet</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Evropéïća is a Latin orthography for Ukrainian. It is designed as a <strong>second script
    alongside Cyrillic</strong>, not as a replacement for it.
  </p>

  <div class="samples">
    {examples.map(([cyr, lat]) => (
      <div class="sample">
        <p class="sample-cyr">{cyr}</p>
        <p class="sample-lat">{lat}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>What it is</h2>

  <p>
    Ukrainian is written in Cyrillic, and has been for a thousand years. Evropéïća adds a
    parallel way of writing the same language in Latin letters, using the Czech and Polish
    diacritic tradition — Č, Š, Ž, Ł, Ń — which Ukrainian already shares with its western
    neighbours through Belarusian Łacinka.
  </p>

  <p>
    Writing is a medium, not the language itself. Serbian is written in both alphabets and is
    no less Serbian for it. Nothing here proposes taking Cyrillic away.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it might matter</h2>

  <ul class="why">
    <li>
      <strong>European integration.</strong> Most of the European Union writes in Latin script.
      A shared alphabet shortens the distance in ordinary things: documents, signage, place
      names, academic publishing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Legibility to neighbours.</strong> Poles, Czechs, Slovaks, Croats and Lithuanians
      read Latin script every day. Ukrainian written this way becomes partly readable to them
      without a dictionary — and Ukrainian words travel into other languages correctly spelled.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Technology and schooling.</strong> Most software and international standards are
      built around the Latin alphabet. One keyboard layout instead of two is simpler, and
      children who write Latin letters early find other European languages easier to learn.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Control of your own names.</strong> Transliteration of Ukrainian names is still
      inconsistent: the same city is spelled several ways across road signs, passports and
      foreign media. An agreed orthography lets Ukrainian export its own spelling instead of
      inheriting someone else's.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is built</h2>

  <p>
    An orthography proposal is usually a document, and documents drift from their own examples.
    This one is executable. The rules live in a
    <a href="https://github.com/evropeica/evropeica.github.io/blob/master/docs/RULEBOOK.md">rulebook</a>
    where each has an identifier; a reference engine implements them; a second implementation in
    another language is cross-checked against the first; and a checker re-transliterates every
    Ukrainian sentence on this website and fails the build if the published text disagrees with
    the engine.
  </p>

  <p>The system is layered, so each version adds rules on top of the last:</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Name</th><th>What it does</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>1.0</strong></td><td>Transliterácija</td>
          <td>Deterministic one-to-one transliteration from modern Cyrillic</td><td>stable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>2.0</strong></td><td>Ortografija</td>
          <td>Etymological spelling of loanwords, restoring the original pronunciation</td><td>in progress</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>2.1</strong></td><td>Nahołos</td>
          <td>Stress marks, written only when stress is not on the first root vowel</td><td>in progress</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Try it</h2>

  <p>
    The <a href={withBase('/converter/')}>converter</a> turns Cyrillic into version 1.0 in real
    time. The <a href={withBase('/spec/v1/')}>specification</a> gives the full alphabet and every
    rule with examples, and there is a
    <a href={withBase('/keyboard/')}>keyboard layout</a> you can install.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>

  <p>
    This is an independent proposal, not a standard and not endorsed by any institution.
    Version 1.0 is stable; versions 2.0 and 2.1 are still moving. Corrections to the rules or the
    examples are welcome as issues or pull requests on
    <a href="https://github.com/evropeica/evropeica.github.io">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <p class="next">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href={withBase('/converter/')}>Open the converter &rarr;</a>
    <a class="btn" href={withBase('/')}>Ukrainian version</a>
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; max-width: 40rem; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  main > p, main > ul { max-width: 40rem; }

  .samples { display: grid; gap: .75rem; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .sample {
    padding: .9rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--paper-raised);
  }
  .sample-cyr { color: var(--ink-faint); font-style: italic; font-size: .95rem; margin-bottom: .2rem; }
  .sample-lat { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0; }

  .why li { margin-bottom: .85rem; }

  .next { display: flex; gap: .6rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .btn {
    font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: .9rem; font-weight: 500;
    padding: .55rem 1.1rem; border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; background: var(--paper-raised);
  }
  .btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .btn-primary { background: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--paper-raised); }
  .btn-primary:hover { background: var(--accent-hover); border-color: var(--accent-hover); color: var(--paper-raised); }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { .btn-primary, .btn-primary:hover { color: #10151a; } }
</style>
